WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.240
Here’s the timeline of the history of Genetics.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.120
Beginning with the work of Gregor Mendel,

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.180
we have reached this point: the experiment

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.200
which proved that it is DNA which is the genetic

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.300
material.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.900
Clearly, the next step would be to understand

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.860
the structure of DNA.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.380
So how is this biomolecule structured?

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.200
What are its constituent molecules?

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.500
Let us find that out in this video!

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.460
The structure of the DNA molecule was discovered

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.720
in the early years of 1950s.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.200
Note that the STRUCTURE was discovered, not

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.280
the molecule!

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.760
The discovery of DNA molecule predates to

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.160
the 18th century.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.680
But DNA was ignored for a long time and it

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.740
was “PROTIENS” that grabbed a lot of attention.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.360
However, post Griffith’s transformation

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.239
experiment, the other two notable works in

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.840
the timeline helped shift the focus towards DNA.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.380
So around the early years of 1950s, an English

00:01:09.382 --> 00:01:12.870
chemist named Rosalind Franklin, started working

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.340
on DNA molecules.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.680
She isolated the DNA crystals and shone X-rays

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:19.520
on them.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.240
As a result, she obtained fine images of the

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.160
structure of DNA molecules.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.180
According to sources, the best one looked

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.160
somewhat like this!

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.460
Around the same time, Francis Crick and James

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.240
Watson, two scientists in the field of genetics,

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.540
worked on the structure of DNA and reached

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:43.500
a similar conclusion like Franklin did!

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.240
However, according to sources, they were fortunate

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.890
to receive better and earlier recognition

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.460
compared to Franklin.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.637
They even shared a Nobel Prize with Maurice

00:01:54.637 --> 00:01:56.960
Wilkins for their discovery of the double

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.900
helical structure of DNA.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.280
Let us understand how exactly this double

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.260
helical structure looks!

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.800
We may have come across this structure of

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:08.860
DNA somewhere.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.440
Is this how it really looks?

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.040
Yes!

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.700
According to Franklin, Watson and Crick, the

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.820
DNA is made up of three major components.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.920
One is a pentose sugar molecule called Deoxyribose!

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.040
How is this obtained?

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.600
It is obtained by removing one oxygen atom

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.620
from a ribose molecule.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.140
Thus the name “Deoxyribonucleic acid”.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.940
The second component is a phosphate group.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:42.380
This helps bind the two deoxyribose molecules

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.140
together!

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.480
The third and fifth carbon atoms of the adjacent

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.060
deoxyribose molecules are bound by the Phosphate

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:51.000
groups!

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.300
Lastly, the nitrogenous bases form the core

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.840
of the double helix!

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.820
These are four different molecules that bind

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:00.720
in pairs.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.220
Two of them are called purines and the other

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.300
two are called pyrimidines.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.140
Adenine and Guanine are the two purines which

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:13.440
bond with the pyrimidines thymine and cytosine.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.099
And can any purine bind with any pyrimidine?

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:18.780
Nope!

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.500
It is a thumb rule that Adenine always binds

00:03:21.503 --> 00:03:25.139
with Thymine; while Guanine binds with cytosine

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.100
only!

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.860
These molecules are bound to each other with

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.640
the help of Hydrogen bonds.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.700
Adenine binds with thymine with the help of

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.140
2 hydrogen bonds.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:40.752
Guanine and cytosine however, bind with three

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:41.930
hydrogen bonds.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.440
Did you know that one unit consisting of a

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.950
deoxyribose molecule, one nitrogenous base

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.540
and a phosphate group is called a “Nucleotide”?

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.140
And what if the phosphate group is removed

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:58.140
from this nucleotide unit?

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.400
It is called a “Nucleoside” then!

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.840
Nora wants to know if there is any way of

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.920
measuring a DNA molecule?

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.260
What about its dimensions?

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.780
Watch the next part to know more!

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:20.580
The dimensions of DNA are very simple and

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.920
easy to remember!

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.640
The diameter of the double helix is around

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.000
2 nanometres.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.980
The double helical strand has one major and

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:34.380
one minor groove that makes up one turn.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.480
The length of one turn is around 3.4 nanometres.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.560
And there are around 10 base pairs in one turn.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.860
Now can you help me with the distance between

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.540
the two adjacent base-pairs?

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.620
It’s simple!

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.220
The distance is zero point three four nanometres!

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.760
It is simply 3.4 divided by 10!!

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.500
This is how the molecules are arranged!

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.340
So in this double helix, this portion is the

00:05:04.345 --> 00:05:07.170
backbone which is made up of the Deoxyribose

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.680
molecules and the phosphate groups.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:13.160
Where as, this portion that forms the core,

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.200
is made up of the nitrogenous bases.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:20.040
Now can you guess the chemical nature of DNA?

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.580
Since it contains nitrogenous “BASES”,

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.320
you would say that DNA is basic in nature,

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.320
right?

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:28.940
But that is not true!

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.820
DNA is in fact, acidic in nature!

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.220
The presence of phosphate groups makes the

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.340
molecule acidic in nature!

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.280
What about its physical appearance?

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:45.180
Can you tell me how LONG can this double helix be?

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.100
Let me tell you an interesting fact!

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.997
If all the DNA from a single cell is pulled

00:05:52.997 --> 00:05:56.500
out and stretched, then it would touch approximately

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.660
2 metres in length!

00:05:58.840 --> 00:05:59.840
Yes!

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:01.360
It’s that long!

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:05.320
But if the DNA stretch is so long, then how

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.720
does it fit into such a tiny nucleus?

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:11.520
We know that a cell is extremely tiny, and

00:06:11.531 --> 00:06:14.539
the nucleus occupies a VERY small portion

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.100
of the tiny cell!

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:19.700
So how does such a long stretch of DNA fit

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.600
into such a tiny space?

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.460
The answer is quite simple!

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:27.640
How do we fit a long telephone wire in a small

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:28.500
space?

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.540
Yes: using the process of COILING!

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:35.520
Similarly, “Coiling” the DNA helps reduce

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.080
its volume and fits it into the tiny nucleus!

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:41.620
But what does it coil around?

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:44.860
Is there any support for this structure?

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:48.160
Or does the long stretch coil around itself?

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.620
Well, there are highly specialised proteins

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.380
that help us in this!

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.479
These are called the Histone proteins!

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.520
A total of five Histone proteins help in the

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.700
wrapping of the DNA molecule!

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:08.580
Histone proteins namely H-1, H-2A, H-2B, H3

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.460
and H4 serve this purpose!

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.380
Among these, the H-2A, H-2B, H3 and H4 come

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.360
in doubles to form a ball like structure which

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:22.200
looks somewhat like this!

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.940
The stretch of DNA then easily wraps around

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:27.380
this octamer formed!

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.600
And what function does the H-1 serve?

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:34.660
Basically the H-1 protein helps in attaching

00:07:34.665 --> 00:07:37.620
the DNA to the octamer and keeping it intact

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:38.780
on place.

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:43.340
In other words, it helps to hold the complete structure!

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:47.520
This unit of the DNA string wrapped on the

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.200
histone core is called “Nucleosome”.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.320
Does this structure resemble something?

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:55.740
Yes!

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:59.840
It looks like a bead having a string wound around it!

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:03.216
That is the reason why the nucleosomes are

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.760
referred to as the “Beads on a string”

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:06.520
structures!

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.100
Any idea what kind of a structure is obtained

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:10.960
from this?

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:14.060
This kind of coiling forms the basic unit

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:17.320
for the formation of the chromatin structure!

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:19.160
Yes!

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.220
We know that chromatin, and thus the chromosomes,

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:23.680
are made up of DNA.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:27.620
So it is this pattern of arrangement of the

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:30.840
DNA that forms coils and helps in the formation

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.240
of the chromatin structure!

